# The working tree must equal the committed bytes on every platform.
#
# Two byte-exact seals depend on it and both were broken by its absence:
# `release/dependency-locks.sha256` hashes Cargo.lock and pnpm-lock.yaml, and
# `MANIFEST.sha256` hashes every delivered source file. GitHub's Windows runners
# set core.autocrlf=true, so a checkout there rewrote every text file to CRLF and
# the freeze recorded hashes of a working tree that exists only on Windows --
# measured in P60 run 34746478525: the artifact's Cargo.lock hash is exactly
# sha256(committed bytes with LF replaced by CRLF).
#
# `-text` rather than `text=auto eol=lf`: no conversion in EITHER direction, so
# the working tree is the committed bytes and nothing is renormalized. eol=lf
# would have rewritten 37 files that are committed with CRLF -- captured Windows
# console output under docs/phase36/evidence/ and one .ps1 -- and the CRLF in
# captured evidence is part of what was captured. It also leaves scripts/*.cmd
# exactly as committed on every platform instead of picking a side.
#
# It does not affect diffs: EOL conversion is the `text` attribute, binary
# detection for diff is content-based and separate.
* -text

# The exception, and the distinction behind it: a file that is CAPTURED keeps
# the bytes it was captured with, and a file that is EXECUTED gets the bytes its
# interpreter needs.
#
# The 37 CRLF files above are captured Windows console output under
# docs/phase36/evidence/ -- the CRLF is part of the evidence, and rewriting it
# would be falsifying a measurement. A .cmd has no such claim on its own bytes.
# cmd.exe is unreliable with LF-only line endings around multi-line constructs,
# and scripts/build-arm64-msi.cmd:91-95 is exactly one of them -- five lines
# joined by a `^` continuation. `* -text` left both .cmd files LF on Windows,
# where core.autocrlf used to deliver them CRLF, so the earlier comment's claim
# that it avoided "picking a side" was wrong: LF is a side, and for a file that
# is executed it is the wrong one.
#
# `eol=crlf` and not autocrlf: it is explicit and platform-INDEPENDENT, so every
# checkout on every OS produces the same CRLF working tree. That is what keeps
# MANIFEST.sha256 verifiable -- the seal hashes the working tree, and a working
# tree that differs by platform is the defect 50cc466 fixed. The index keeps LF;
# the working tree is CRLF everywhere, which is a different thing from
# autocrlf's "CRLF on Windows, LF elsewhere".
*.cmd text eol=crlf
